<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Recycle Our Paper! – Lesson # 3</h1>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> An Environmental Project</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Recycle Our Paper!</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Explain structured rules for specific activities, like playing a game, or collaborating on a project. interactive: Coordinate roles and tasks for a group project.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain rules for recycling (e.g., 'Everyone needs to bring their own bag.'). Can coordinate roles for the environmental project with classmates (e.g., 'You bring markers, and I'll bring paper.'). Can ask and answer logistics questions on recycling task (e.g., 'Do we wash bottles before putting them in recycling?').</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage aims to engage students, activate prior knowledge, and introduce the target language in a clear and modeled way, ensuring comprehension.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of items: paper, plastic bottle, glass bottle, food waste. Teacher asks, "What can we recycle?" Students call out "Paper!" or "Plastic!" as the teacher points to the corresponding flashcard. The teacher then focuses on the 'paper' flashcard and says, "Today, we will talk about our 'Let's Recycle Our Paper!' project."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a piece of paper and a blue recycling bin. The teacher models a simple instruction sequence for recycling paper: "First, <b>bring</b> your paper. Then, <b>put</b> it in the blue bin." Teacher repeats, emphasizing the imperatives and using gestures. Teacher models the pronunciation of 'will' /wɪl/ and 'I'll' /aɪl/ by saying, "I <b>will</b> recycle. You <b>will</b> recycle. We'<b>ll</b> recycle." The teacher also models segmenting 'pre-sen-ta-tion' into syllables.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we put plastic bottles in the *blue bin* for paper? (No!)"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'bring your paper' an instruction or a question? (An instruction!)"</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage introduces the target language in a meaningful context, allowing students to observe its use in an authentic scenario related to the theme.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated dialogue between two students, Ana and Luis, planning their "Let's Recycle Our Paper!" project. The dialogue is written on the board or on a large chart paper.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short dialogue for a planning meeting (classroom discussion format), using simple future tense for plans and imperatives for instructions. The dialogue includes vocabulary such as 'project,' 'plan,' 'meeting,' 'classroom,' 'bring,' 'clean,' 'participate.'</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue:</b><br>
          <span style="font-weight: bold;">Ana:</span> "Hi Luis! Are you ready for our recycling <b>project</b> <b>meeting</b> today?"<br>
          <span style="font-weight: bold;">Luis:</span> "Yes, Ana! I'm very <b>excited</b>. What is our <b>plan</b>?"<br>
          <span style="font-weight: bold;">Ana:</span> "First, we <b>will</b> discuss the rules for recycling paper in our <b>classroom</b>. Everyone <b>needs to bring</b> their own bag for paper."<br>
          <span style="font-weight: bold;">Luis:</span> "Okay. So, '<b>Bring</b> your clean paper here.' What else?"<br>
          <span style="font-weight: bold;">Ana:</span> "Then, we <b>will</b> assign roles. You <b>will</b> be in charge of labeling the bins. I'<b>ll</b> <b>organize</b> the paper collection schedule. We <b>will</b> <b>participate</b> together!"<br>
          <span style="font-weight: bold;">Luis:</span> "Perfect! We <b>will</b> <b>clean</b> our environment!"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Give and follow simple instructions and suggestions related to classroom activities and environmental actions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "What project are Ana and Luis planning?" (Gist question: 'Let's Recycle Our Paper!')</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True or False: "Luis will label the bins." (True)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True or False: "They will recycle plastic bottles in this project." (False)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on developing accuracy in speaking by practicing the target grammar and vocabulary through structured, controlled activities.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking: Explaining rules and coordinating tasks using Future simple tense ('We will plant trees,' 'I'll bring markers'), Imperatives ('Bring a shovel,' 'Put paper in the blue bin'), and Present simple to describe actions ('We wash bottles'). Focus on pronunciation of 'will' /wɪl/ and 'I'll' /aɪl/ and segmenting multi-syllabic words like 'presentation.'</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Gap-fill & Oral Repetition):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks. Students orally complete them using imperatives or simple future based on context. Example: "Everyone ____ (bring) their paper bags." (Students say: "Everyone *bring* their paper bags.") "We ____ (clean) the classroom next week." (Students say: "We *will clean* the classroom next week.") The teacher corrects pronunciation of 'will' and 'I'll' as needed.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Builder):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives sentence starters about the project. Students complete them orally, focusing on their roles and future plans. Example starters: "I will ____ (verb) the ____ (noun)." "You will ____ (verb) ____." Pairs share their sentences. (e.g., "I will *bring* the *big bags*." "You will *collect* the *paper*.") Teacher monitors for use of target vocabulary like 'project,' 'plan,' 'review,' 'complete,' 'present,' 'finish,' 'submit.'</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Giving Instructions):</b> In pairs, students take turns giving instructions to their partner for "preparing a recycling station." One student says an imperative, and the other pretends to do it. E.g., Student A: "<b>Bring</b> a blue bin." Student B: (Mimes bringing a bin) "Okay, I'll bring a blue bin." Student A: "<b>Put</b> a 'Paper Only' sign on it." Student B: (Mimes putting a sign) "Done!" They can ask logistics questions: "Do we wash bottles before putting them in recycling?" (Even if it's a paper project, this helps integrate the full LO).</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage encourages students to use the language freely and collaboratively to complete a communicative task, focusing on fluency and achieving the specific objective.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will engage in a small group role-play simulating a "Project Planning Meeting" for "Let's Recycle Our Paper!" The teacher divides the class into groups of 3-4 students.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Role-Play. Each group will pretend they are meeting to plan their recycling project. They must coordinate roles and explain the rules for collecting paper. They will use the target vocabulary and grammar (future simple for plans, imperatives for rules, present simple for descriptions).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Instructions for students:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In your group, imagine you are starting the 'Let's Recycle Our Paper!' <b>project</b>."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"First, decide who <b>will</b> do what. Use sentences like, 'I'<b>ll bring</b> the labels,' or 'You <b>will collect</b> the paper.' (Students use vocabulary like 'bring,' 'collect,' 'organize.')"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Next, discuss two important rules for recycling paper. Use imperatives: '<b>Clean</b> your paper first,' or '<b>Put</b> paper in the blue bin.'"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Finally, ask and answer at least one logistics question about the task. For example, 'Do we need to separate cardboard?' or 'When is the <b>due date</b> for our first collection?'"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Be ready to share your plan with the class. Remember to pronounce 'will' and 'I'll' clearly."</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, observing, and providing assistance as students actively discuss environmental actions and coordinate their roles.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage allows for formative assessment of student learning and provides an opportunity for students to reflect on their performance and learning process.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the group role-play and a brief oral check-in with each group.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their recycling project plan to the class (1-2 minutes). They must state at least one rule and one assigned task. Students also ask and answer one logistics question from the teacher or another group.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially 'will' /wɪl/, 'I'll' /aɪl/).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., 'project,' 'plan,' 'bring,' 'clean').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses future simple for plans (e.g., 'We will collect').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses imperatives for rules (e.g., 'Bring paper').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Coordinates roles and tasks (e.g., 'You bring markers, I'll bring paper.').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asks/answers logistics questions (e.g., 'Do we separate colors?').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Communicative clarity and collaboration within the group.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral or Quick Write):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One rule for our paper recycling <b>project</b> is..."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I <b>will do</b> to help our <b>environment</b> is..."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages critical thinking about the lesson's effectiveness and plans for future learning, ensuring continuous improvement.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining the rules or coordinating tasks was easy for you today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you need more practice with when talking about our <b>project</b>?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this lesson, especially regarding student speaking and interaction? The students were very engaged in the role-play and used imperatives effectively. The modeling of 'will' and 'I'll' was helpful for pronunciation. The prompt for asking logistics questions worked well to encourage interactive speaking. What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled to use the future simple tense consistently for plans without prompting. I need to provide more sentence frames for future simple during the practice stage next time. Also, segmenting multisyllabic words like 'presentation' could use more explicit practice in the warm-up of the next lesson.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This speaking lesson prepared students to explain rules and coordinate tasks for the "Let's Recycle Our Paper!" project. In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will use the same vocabulary and grammar to write a short set of instructions for their project and write about their assigned roles, building on the oral practice from today.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>